  {
    "episode_id": 95642,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95642.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "I got to go back to, you know, I wrote a book in 2000 and I think of 11, 12, I started writing a book. on mindfulness meditation, believe it or not. And I was doing a bunch of research about stress on the body. And I started to really see that what was really debilitating for the body was stress, but it was also diet. And so then I got into a whole initiative around writing a second book around food, and the book's called The Real Food Revolution. And as I was doing research for the real food revolution, I got into kind of the agriculture piece of the food and how our food was affecting our health. But you really couldn't do any research on health and food without getting into agriculture. So that began the process of me learning about how we're losing bees as pollinators, what was happening to a runoff that I was seeing in Ohio with the algae blooms on the Great Lakes. We've been dealing with that problem for a while. And so the idea of a new and better way of doing agriculture became very apparent to me that that was something really important. And then I just got interested in it. I mean, I'm from like a Rust Belt congressional district with a Youngstown, Ohio, Akron, Ohio. to represent Ohio, so we do have ag interests in Ohio, very much old school, but also learn more and more about some of these specialty crops that are just fruit vegetables that we call girls special now, all of a sudden. And then I started meeting more people who introduced me to people like Gabe Brown. So I immediately bought Gabe's book and, you know, read his book and had conversations with Gabe and Alan Williams. And in my book, The Real Food Revolution, I talked a lot about urban agriculture, about what Will Allen's doing and places like Milwaukee and how we can build out the urban ag too is an opportunity to simulate development. So anyway, and now I watch videos and All my ag friends are sending me all kinds of good stuff to read so I'm super pumped about it because I think the country is looking for new solutions and I think when they hear about this kind of things we're going to talk and makes a lot of sense.",
