    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95642.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, of course. I think most people that are watching this will know a lot, probably a lot, in the last six months to a year to really understand what's happening around the regenerative agriculture and the idea that the soil is never really dead. It can come back alive if the proper techniques are used. And so we all see what's happening with the industrial egg system that we have now. Obviously, there's monopolies this industry, we see it all over the country, but I've learned a lot about that in Iowa out campaigning there. We see the algae blooms, we see the dead zone at the mouth of the Mississippi River, and you said there's 200 plus around the world. And Dr. Mark Hyman, I was with him the other day, and he was telling me that there's 220,000, 120,000. 220,220 metric tons of fish that are killed every year at the mouth of the Mississippi River in that dead zone there. So it's a really good healthy lean protein and it's the fertilizer runoff. that's happening because the ground so hard when it rains it goes into the rivers and rushes down there. So we have the algae blooms, we have the soil that's been destroyed, we have these huge dead zones where it's killing fish, wrecking the fishing into to fund these problems, I'm tired of us always trying to put the band-aid on something, right? So how do we deal with the dead zone? How do we deal with farmers who aren't making money? How do we deal with algae blooms? How do we deal with the fishing industry that's being hurt? I want us to get to the root cause of these problems and that means moving to regenerative ag, reducing the pesticide use, reducing the fertilizer use, which will around drought and flooding, and obviously sequester carbon, which is a big initiative for climate, so benefit, benefit, benefit, benefit, and the farmers are making money versus destroy the environment, destroy the rivers, the farmers and the economy and the overall America. It's all really tied together.",
