    "turn_text": "I don't know. I get so excited when I hear things like that. Because I mean, I'm 46 years old. And we have three kids. So Mason's 16, Bella's 15, and Brady, our little guy's five. And I think about what we want to do to reverse climate change and really put systems in place that are effective and real solutions. And to do it in a way where a lot of people, if you start saying, well, don't eat meat or ban meat or those kind of things, well, a lot of people don't want to stop eating meat. But I think if we can come in with this idea of, look, this is actually how things used to be. This is how we build out that old system that used to happen here that is good for the environment and good for our health. uh, you know, negative footprint. I mean, that's amazing. So let's do it. And so what I want is the federal government and the most powerful government in the world to lead on these things, to really show the initiatives, put the power, the weight, the resources of the federal government behind these things. And what I did learn from from Gabe and Alan Williams is that Like we can literally get to a point where we can start reducing subsidies for farmers because they're all making money and they talk to Gabe and he says, I don't even take the subsidies because he's making money and learning that this can really happen within like a year or two. The regenerative farmers can start making money. So how do we build that system out and do, you know, Gabe and Allen are doing as far as building teams out in each state to really help farmers learn how to do this in a very quick way. Is there something that we would need to do from the federal government side to help the transition with farmers who may have made some significant capital investments or made investments for the old system? and others about how we would do that and actually do it in a very thoughtful way. But think about reducing the hundreds of billions of dollars that we spend on subsidies, crop insurance, and all of these things, and all of these programs. If we could start reducing that, the money we would have to reinvest back in the rural America polluted for renovating theaters and building up the downtowns with river walks and amphitheaters and bike trails. Like everywhere I go, you know, and I represent a lot of these communities. They have this. beautiful little downtown is a great courthouse. There's an old movie theater that the community's trying to get renovated. So if we can reduce the subsidies to the farmers, they're making more money than they ever have. Hopefully, if we build the system up and then take money and help broadband and, you know, in these local communities, we use that money to build that back up to me. That's a very unifying approach that's not asking for more money, but reducing money and kind of rearranging on how we invested in rural America.",
