    "turn_idx": 81,
    "turn_text": "Again, holistic, right? Holistic solutions to the problems that we have. So we spend two and a half times as much on health care as every other industrialized country and we get the worst results. And about 75% of those health care costs are for chronic diseases that are largely preventable. And a lot of those diseases, heart disease, high blood diet-related inflammation. Type 2 diabetes has almost everything to do with diet. And these things are reversible. You can reduce inflammation in your body through your diet and stress reduction as we started this whole thing out. Those are the two real killers, quite frankly, and they cause inflammation to your body. So your body inflames, and then it inflames your heart. You get heart disease inflames, your body, and you get high blood pressure. You get disease, disease, your body's not doing well. One of the solutions to reducing healthcare costs and reversing things like type 2 diabetes is using food as a medicine. And we have seen this happening now across the United States in different pockets where a diabetic in half the country now has either diabetes or pre-diabetes, half the adult population. And a diabetic costs us about $14,000 a year for type 2 diabetes. And so initiatives like foodism to diabetes and getting people off of their medications. I met a woman in Iowa who lost 90 pounds. She just happened to fall into a program that was doing food as medicine with other supports as well. For 5,000 bucks one time you can reverse type two diabetes. and instead of $14,000 a year every single year to manage type 2 diabetes. But what do we need? We need healthy food and we need it to be readily available everywhere, not just prevent type 2 diabetes by making sure that this healthy food that comes from regenerative agriculture is readily available to people. And that's why I also want to build out of a huge urban agriculture program in our cities that have shrunk from, you know, a couple hundred thousand people down to 50, 60, 70,000 people. How do we clear out those old dilapidated homes, build up urban agriculture, create summer jobs, create afterschool jobs. How do we tie the food as medicine to the school curriculum in our schools? How do we have school gardens? How do we have salad bars in our schools? How do we have literally have kitchens that kids can learn how to cook?",
